  "assembly": "Post-loop (#2506 pins, restated for context): the two taken entries' words are staged at [rsp+0x208]/[rsp+0x210] (key 0) and [rsp+0x218]/[rsp+0x220] (key 1); each assembled 0x40-byte record is built at [rsp+0x20] with the taken words at its +0x30/+0x38 slots and appended to a vector (grow 0x474e420 through slots 0x64e1470/0x64e1478); a third record built from saved words is appended to the second vector of the working pair at [rsp+0x1d0]; at 0x15a54c4..0x15a54ec the updated pair (0x30 bytes) is stored back to its home at [rsp+0x240] before the tree drop at 0x15a54fc and the drain region.",
  "drain_verdict": "The drain region (0x15a5501..0x15a5861) never reads the assembled records.  A stack census over the whole consumer body shows zero drain-region accesses to the pair home [rsp+0x240..0x268], the working pair [rsp+0x1d0..0x1f8], or the taken-word slots [rsp+0x208..0x220].  The drain works on a different map (handle words at [rsp+0x178..0x188], cursors at [rsp+0x28]/[rsp+0x48]) through the iterator 0x153ba90 and on the 0x18-byte-element vector at [rsp+0x90].  Its single call to the helper 0x149e5b0 (site 0x15a569e, arguments rdi = &[rsp+0xc0], rsi = &[rsp+0x28]) does not touch the records either: the helper loads only its cursor argument's three words, walks map-#1 nodes by the count words at node +0x110/+0x112 and the child link at node +0x120, frees nodes with the size class 0x120 (first) or 0x180 (deeper) through the dealloc slot, and writes a fresh 0x30-byte cursor {next node, 0, remainder, node, index, count} to its out argument; the consumer stores that cursor over its cursor-1 slot (0x15a56ea..0x15a56ee) and loops.  No 0x40-byte record field is loaded anywhere in the helper.",
  "exit_verdict": "On the normal path the saved pair is read exactly once, at the consumer exit block (0x15a58f2): the consumer builds an empty vector {0, 8, 0} at [rsp+0xc0] and calls the exit reader 0x4749ab0 through slot 0x64e1480 with rdi = &[rsp+0x20] (out), rsi = &[rsp+0x240] (the saved pair), rdx = &[rsp+0xc0].  The reader first calls its delegate 0x4748500 through slot 0x64eafd8 to fill a 0x68-byte temp from the pair.  If the temp word at +0x60 is non-zero, the reader writes out = {6, temp+0x50, temp+0x58, temp+0x60} (0x20 bytes) and destroys the temp's pair copy inline: first-vector elements through the element drop 0x474dcd0, its buffer freed at capacity * 0x40, second-vector elements freed inline (the +0x8/+0x10 allocation and the +0x28 0x18-byte sub-vector of each).  If the word is zero, the reader copies the temp's first 0x50 bytes verbatim to out -- the pair copy moves out with it -- and frees only the delegate's 0x20-byte entry vector buffer (count << 5).  Back in the consumer, an out word of 6 branches to the assertion arm at 0x15a5b8c (slot 0x64df6a0, target 0x1680c30); otherwise the consumer copies the 0x50-byte out struct to the destination pointer loaded from [rsp+0x278] and returns.",
  "delegate_verdict": "The delegate 0x4748500 (5,545 bytes, hash-pinned boundary; entry loops pinned here) consumes both vectors of the pair.  It copies the second vector's header in at the entry (words from pair +0x18/+0x28 to its local at [rsp+0x240]) and runs the same transform loop over each vector in turn (first-vector loop head at 0x47485d4, second-vector loop head at 0x4748c47): for every 0x40-byte element whose first word is not the sentinel 0x8000000000000000, it stages the element at [rsp+0x170] (word 0 verbatim, word at +0x38 re-staged at +0x30), copies the element's string (length word at +0x8 region, pointer at +0x10 region of the staged record) through alloc + memcpy, gates the element through the transform gate 0x474ba60, and on a true result appends a 0x20-byte entry {0, element word 0, length, pointer} to a fresh local vector (grow 0x474e490 through slot 0x64eafe0); the element's tagged pair (staged at [rsp+0x1a0]/[rsp+0x1a8]) is then destroyed through its descriptor.  At the end it writes the entry vector's header to the temp at +0x50/+0x60 (count at +0x60, 0x47494d6) -- the words the exit reader's discriminant-6 arm extracts.  The delegate's later merge phases are not traced.",
  "disposition_verdict": "Final disposition of the extracted words.  On unwind paths the taken words never reach the records: the consumer's landing pads destroy each taken pair directly through a tagged destroy -- 0x158bee0 at 0x15a5dfa (key-1 words) and 0x15a5e4c (key-0 words), and its byte-twin 0x15ad360 at 0x15a6186 / 0x15a61a3 -- which calls descriptor word 0 (drop) on the +0x8 word when non-null and frees it through the dealloc slot with the class from descriptor words +0x8 (size) and +0x10 (align, tzcnt).  The assembled records themselves are destroyed by the pair drop 0x15ac5f0, called on the working pair at [rsp+0x1d0] (0x15a5eb2), on the pair at [rsp+0x90] (0x15a5e14), on the pair in the [rsp+0xc0] scratch (0x15a60d9) and on the saved pair at [rsp+0x240] (0x15a64d4, the final cleanup before _Unwind_Resume): it drops every first-vector element through the element drop 0x1538ec0 stepping 0x40 from the buffer, frees that buffer at capacity * 0x40, then destroys every second-vector element inline -- freeing the allocation at element +0x10 with size element +0x8, dropping the 0x18-byte sub-vector at element +0x28 (each sub-element's allocation freed, the sub-buffer freed at count * 0x18) -- and frees the second buffer at capacity * 0x40 through the pair tail helper 0x15ad260.  On the normal path the words' ownership passes out of the consumer inside the records (exit verdict); no other consumer of the +0x30/+0x38 slots exists in the consumer body (stack census) or in any direct caller of the pinned helpers (call census).",
  "limits": "Static trace only.  The merge delegate 0x4748500 is a hash-pinned boundary beyond its two entry loops and its temp writes: its later merge phases, the transform gate 0x474ba60, the element drops 0x1538ec0 / 0x474dcd0, the pair tail helper 0x15ad260, the drain iterator 0x153ba90, the grow functions 0x474e420 / 0x474e490 and the assertion entry 0x1680c30 are hash-pinned boundaries.  What the delegate's entry vector means semantically, why the discriminant-6 arm is an assertion in the consumer, and what the destination object at [rsp+0x278] does with the 0x50-byte result are not claimed.  The two tagged-destroy instances are claimed identical only at their pinned instruction shape.  No captured code executed, no runtime value or reconstruction gate is claimed.",
